**Expected behavior**

The loop prints five finite, decreasing losses and `completed without corruption`. When a cached autotune winner fails launch validation, the tuner should fall back to another candidate (or re-tune) rather than panic; and a failed op on the device-runner thread should fail the stream loudly rather than leave registered-but-never-written tensor handles behind.

**Additional context**

*The failing problem* (instrumented the validator to print it): the forward head matmul `[512,128] @ [128,7]`, rhs strides `[7,1]` → canonical stride 28 bytes, under the 16-byte async-copy threshold checked by `validate_async_copy_with_problem` (`cubek-matmul/src/components/global/read/strategy/base.rs`).

*Defect 1 — the autotune key overstates stride alignment.* `MatmulAutotuneKey` computes `lhs_stride_factor` / `rhs_stride_factor` from the **anchored** dims, not the real strides (`cubek-matmul/src/strategy/tune_key.rs`: `stride_factor(k_anchored, …)`). `anchor(7) = 8` keys this problem as 32-byte aligned; `anchor(126) = 128` keys a 504-byte-stride problem as 512-byte aligned. Problems that fail the async-copy/TMA validators therefore share buckets with problems that pass them, and a winner tuned on an aligned member is an invalid config for the others — validated only *after* autotune has committed. (Our real workload's autotune cache shows these collision buckets directly: the `Linear(126, _)` forward/backward problems share buckets with the 128-wide hidden-layer ones.)

*Defect 2 — nothing gates these strategies on alignment, so an invalid config can win autotune.* `Strategy::SpecializedCyclicCmma` / `SpecializedCyclicMma` read through `AsyncPartialCyclicLoading`, but they are registered in the `accelerated` group of `burn-cubecl/src/kernel/matmul/tune/base.rs` with no alignment gate — while the `tma` group applies exactly the `stride_factor >= 4` threshold these need. An under-aligned problem therefore hands them `PRIORITY_MAX`, and the failure only appears at setup, after autotune has committed to the winner, where `.expect("Should run when selected by autotune.")` in `cubecl-runtime/src/tune/local.rs` turns it into a panic. The fix belongs in selection, not in recovery — see the comment below for the full root cause.

*Defect 3 — the panic is swallowed and the stream keeps going.* Fire-and-forget tasks on the device-runner thread run under `catch_unwind` and only `log::warn!` the payload (`cubecl-common/src/device/handle/channel.rs`). Outputs are registered before execution, so a panicked op leaves live, never-written tensor handles behind — this is the silent-NaN mechanism, and it is unchanged in pre.2 (only blocking calls propagate errors, via the oneshot `CallError` path). A failed task should poison the stream/queue loudly.

PRs: defect 1 (key reports real stride alignment) — https://github.com/tracel-ai/cubek/pull/488; defect 2 (gate the async-copy strategies on that alignment) — see the comment below. They are only meaningful together: the gate reads the stride factors off the key, so it cannot fire until the key stops deriving them from the anchored dims.

An earlier attempt at defect 2 made the tuner fall back to the remaining candidates on a launch failure (cubecl#1510). That was rejected upstream, correctly: it would have to hand the real inputs to a second candidate, and autotune must never clone them or in-place operations write into the copy. Preventing the selection is the right layer.

