## Describe the bug

On the CUDA backend, `burn::backend::Cuda` (which is
`Fusion<CubeBackend<CudaRuntime, …>>`) produces a **numerically wrong** result for
the *batched* forward pass of a small MLP whose head narrows to a single output
column (`… -> Linear(_, 1) -> tanh`, output shape `[N, 1]`).

The **same model** with the **same weights**, run on the **same GPU** but with the
`Fusion<…>` wrapper removed (`CubeBackend<CudaRuntime, …>` directly), matches the
CPU `NdArray` oracle to f32 noise. So does the fused backend when the batch is fed
**one row at a time** (`[1, 1]` output). Only the *fused + batched + narrow-output*
combination is wrong.

The error is large (~1e-1 to ~1e0, on outputs bounded to `(-1, 1)` by `tanh`) and
**deterministic** run-to-run — a fusion correctness bug, not drift or
non-determinism. Its signature is distinctive: **row 0 is correct, every later row
is wrong.**

