## Isolation

One-variable cuts narrowing to fusion:

| Experiment | Result | Rules out |
|---|---|---|
| Bare `[N,9] · [9,1]` matmul (no module), fused CUDA vs NdArray | **bit-exact** | raw matmul codegen / environment |
| Full MLP, fused CUDA, one row at a time (`[1,1]`) | correct | needs batch N > 1 |
| A **wide**-headed net (`… -> Linear(_, 9)`), fused CUDA, batched | correct | specific to the narrow `[N,1]` tail |
| `CUBECL_AUTOTUNE_LEVEL=0` (minimal autotune) | still wrong, same values | autotune / kernel selection |
| Full MLP on **un-fused** `CubeBackend`, batched | correct | **⇒ the `Fusion` layer is the cause** |

All three conditions are required together: the **composed module** forward, **batch
N > 1**, and a **narrow `[N, 1]`** output tail. The "row 0 correct, rows 1..N wrong"
signature suggests the fused kernel indexes/strides the narrow output dimension
incorrectly across the batch.

## Workaround

Use the un-fused backend where such a forward's correctness matters:

```rust
type Backend = burn_cubecl::CubeBackend<cubecl::cuda::CudaRuntime, f32, i32, u8>;
```

Widening the head so it never emits `[N, 1]` (e.g. `Linear(_, 2)`, take column 0)
also avoids it — consistent with the narrow-tail diagnosis.

